# ankitools

Scripts and files for my Anki study of Mandarin. Some large dictionary
sources are excluded from git repo but noted in dict/README.

## multizhdict

Segments a string of Chinese characters into words and prints one Chinese
and one English definition for each:

    ./multizhdict 我愛北京天安門
    echo 我愛北京天安門 | ./multizhdict

`multizhdict` is a wrapper that pipes `gomultizhdict` into `less -S`, which
truncates long lines rather than wrapping them so that the columns stay
lined up; scroll sideways with the arrow keys. Run `gomultizhdict` directly
to send the output anywhere else. Input may be traditional or simplified:
both headword forms of every entry are matched.

### Building

    make                 # builds bin/gomultizhdict
    make test
    make install         # PREFIX=/usr/local

Go 1.21 or later and nothing outside its standard library.

The binary finds the dictionaries by looking for a `dict` directory at or
above its own location, and then at or above the working directory. An
installed copy sits outside this checkout and so finds neither: point
`MULTIZHDICT_DICT` at the absolute path of `dict/`, or pass `-dict`.

### Output

Two lines per word, separated from the next word by a rule as wide as the
terminal. Every line carries the same fields in the same order — word,
dictionary, traditional, simplified, pinyin, definition — padded into
columns by display width, with blanks where a dictionary has nothing for a
field. `-tsv` emits single tabs instead, for feeding something else.

The first line of each pair is the Chinese definition and the second is the
English one. Each comes from the first of its two sources that has the word:

| line    | primary source                           | fallback                  |
| ------- | ---------------------------------------- | ------------------------- |
| Chinese | dict/MoEDICT/MoEDict-05-Simp03-cards.txt | dict/ccdict_additions.tsv |
| English | dict/cedict_parsed.tsv                   | dict/cedict_additions.tsv |

A line reading `(no 中文)` or `(no English)` is a word that no source on
that side has. `-all` prints every matching entry rather than the first per
side, which is how to see the other readings of a character like 行.

### Segmentation

| -seg | segmentation                                           |
| ---- | ------------------------------------------------------ |
| fmm  | forward maximum matching: longest word from the left   |
| bmm  | backward maximum matching: longest word from the right |
| bidi | both, then the tie-break below (the default)           |

Maximum matching takes the longest dictionary word starting at the current
position, then resumes after it, falling back to a single character where no
longer word is known. Run forward it errs in a predictable direction:
wherever a long word and a shorter one share a prefix it takes the long one,
so 研究生命起源 comes out as 研究生 / 命 / 起源. Run backward it makes a
different set of mistakes, and the two agree except where the run is
genuinely ambiguous. `bidi` runs both and prefers the result with fewer
words, then the one with fewer single-character words, then the backward
one.

A unigram-frequency model — what jieba does — would beat all three, but none
of these dictionaries carries word frequencies, so there is nothing to score
competing segmentations with.

Runs are segmented between non-Chinese characters, which are dropped. A
full-width comma and an interpunct are exceptions, because 1,469 entries
have a comma in a headword (couplets and proverbs) and 197 have an
interpunct (transliterated names); both are skipped when no word covers
them.

### Other scripts

`add_words_ccdict.py` builds Anki rows for a list of words, prompting for
anything the dictionaries lack. `scripts/` holds one-off column fixes.
